TITLE = "Investment gestation lags, intangible capital and the measurement of capital and productivity in 39 economies"
ABSTRACT = [
("Purpose", "Growth accounting usually builds capital with the perpetual inventory method (PIM), treats the investment-to-output lag as constant or negligible and leaves much intangible investment outside produced capital. This paper asks how much these conventions matter for measured capital, total factor productivity (TFP) and labour shares across countries."),
("Design/methodology/approach", "We estimate time-varying gestation lags and country-specific intangible shares for 39 economies over 1970–2019 using Penn World Table, World Bank and OECD data, discipline them jointly with World Bank Changing Wealth of Nations produced-capital series (1995–2019) and build a parameter-free observable lag from OECD asset composition for a 35-country subsample."),
("Findings", "Under calibrated asset lags, the observable correction lowers 2010–2019 capital by a median 4.3% (country-bootstrap 95% interval −5.0% to −3.9%) and raises log-level TFP and the implied labour share by about 1.7 percentage points. A time-varying lag lowers median out-of-sample error from 4.60% to 3.99%, but the difference is not statistically significant (Wilcoxon p = 0.18). The joint flow–stock model improves a strict wealth-account holdout."),
("Originality", "The paper combines time-varying gestation, intangible capital and a wealth-account consistency check in one cross-country accounting framework. The adjustments are assumption-conditional measurement scenarios, not direct evidence of true capital mismeasurement."),
]
KEYWORDS = "Time-to-build, Intangible capital, Capital stock measurement, Total factor productivity, Growth accounting, Labour share"
PAPER_TYPE = "Research paper"
JEL = "E01, E22, O47"

S = {}
S[4] = "1. Introduction"
S[5] = ("The composition of investment has shifted. Across advanced and middle-income economies, the share of gross fixed capital formation (GFCF) in plant and machinery has declined, while software, research and development (R&D) and other intellectual-property products have risen since the 1980s (OECD, 2013; Corrado et al., 2020). These assets differ both in whether they are recorded as produced capital and in how long they take to become productive. Growth-accounting capital series are usually constructed with a perpetual inventory method (PIM) in which investment enters the productive stock with a fixed and short, often zero, delay, and broad intangibles are only partly capitalised. Because total factor productivity (TFP) is a residual, any systematic mis-timing or omission in capital is absorbed into measured TFP (Griliches, 1996).")
S[6] = ("We ask how much these conventions matter for measured capital, TFP and labour shares in a cross-country panel. The paper makes three contributions. First, it estimates a time-varying investment-to-output lag ⟦μ(t)⟧ for 39 economies over 1970–2019. Second, it recovers a country-specific intangible share ⟦β⟧ by jointly fitting production-side growth residuals and produced-capital trajectories from the World Bank Changing Wealth of Nations (CWON). Third, it constructs an observable, parameter-free lag ⟦M_{obs}⟧ from OECD asset composition and literature-based asset gestation lags, so that the measurement consequences can be computed without estimation. Methodologically, we borrow the tempo-and-quantum logic used to adjust period fertility for drifting timing (Bongaarts and Feeney, 1998).")
S[7] = None
S[8] = ("Time-to-build has a long history in macroeconomics (Kydland and Prescott, 1982), and empirical work has documented lag heterogeneity across regimes and industries (Mayer, 1960; Koeva, 2000; Kaboski, 2005). Our question is narrower and empirical: whether drift in the average lag, driven by changing asset composition, produces measurable differences in standard cross-country capital and TFP series, and whether a wealth-account constraint helps separate this timing effect from an omitted intangible component.")
S[9] = None
S[10] = ("The corrections are modest but systematic. In the 35 economies with OECD asset data, the observable lag lowers the 2010–2019 average capital stock by a median 4.3% relative to the standard PIM, and all 35 country means are negative. Mechanically, measured log-level TFP and the implied labour share rise by a median 1.7 percentage points. The largest adjustments occur in economies whose investment mix moved most towards long-gestation assets, such as Ireland (−9.2%) and the Republic of Korea (−7.1%). The forecasting evidence is weaker: a time-varying lag lowers median out-of-sample error from 4.60% to 3.99%, but the paired difference is not statistically significant. On the wealth side, a strict 2015–2019 holdout gives a lower median residual for the joint flow–stock model than for production-only estimates.")
S[11] = ("These results matter for cross-country productivity comparisons. Where investment composition differs across countries, a common zero-lag convention can affect comparisons of capital deepening, TFP and labour shares. The adjustments are conditional on calibrated asset lags and on CWON as a reference account; they are measurement-sensitivity scenarios rather than direct proof that official capital stocks are wrong.")
S[12] = ("Section 2 reviews related work, Section 3 sets out the framework, Section 4 describes data and methods, Section 5 reports results, Section 6 discusses interpretation and limitations and Section 7 concludes. The supplementary material reports robustness checks.")
S[13] = "2. Related literature"
S[14] = ("__Time-to-build and capital measurement.__ Since Kydland and Prescott (1982), multi-period investment lags have been standard in business-cycle models, with stochastic and time-to-plan extensions (Altug, 1989; Christiano and Todd, 1996; Edge, 2007). Empirical estimates typically identify a lag for a whole sample or for a small number of regimes (Mayer, 1960; Koeva, 2000), and Kaboski (2005) documents cross-industry heterogeneity. Growth-accounting practice has long recognised that capital-measurement conventions shape the residual (Jorgenson and Griliches, 1967; Hulten, 1992; Jorgenson et al., 2005; Inklaar and Timmer, 2013). We are not aware of cross-country growth-accounting work that lets the average lag drift with asset composition and traces the consequences for capital, TFP and wealth-account consistency jointly; this is the specific gap we address.")
S[15] = ("__Intangible capital.__ Corrado et al. (2005, 2009) showed that software, R&D, design, brand, organisational capital and training are quantitatively large, and the INTAN-Invest programme extended this evidence internationally (Corrado et al., 2016; Roth, 2022). Capitalising intangibles changes measured productivity growth (dal Borgo et al., 2013; Corrado et al., 2017), and McGrattan and Prescott (2010) and Haskel and Westlake (2017) emphasise the bias from unmeasured investment when the intangible share grows. The 2008 SNA capitalised R&D (United Nations et al., 2009), but broader intangibles remain largely outside official balance sheets, including CWON (Lange et al., 2021). In this journal, Castelli et al. (2024) relate tangible and intangible assets to labour productivity growth, and Çelik (2021) studies how human capital affects labour income shares at the sectoral level. Our contribution is complementary: we examine how timing and coverage conventions in capital construction propagate to aggregate TFP and labour-share measurement.")
S[16] = ("__Wealth accounting.__ The Beyond-GDP agenda proposes wealth-based aggregates alongside GDP (Stiglitz et al., 2009; Arrow et al., 2012; Jorgenson, 2018; Managi and Kumar, 2018; Dasgupta, 2021). Produced-capital series from wealth accounts and from PIM-based productivity databases are constructed under different conventions and need not agree. We use CWON produced capital as a second, partly independent reference account, not as ground truth.")
S[17] = ("__Tempo adjustment in demography.__ Bongaarts and Feeney (1998) showed that a drifting mean age at childbearing distorts period fertility even when completed fertility is unchanged, and later work refined and tested the adjustment (Kohler et al., 2002; Goldstein et al., 2003; Bongaarts and Sobotka, 2012). We use this logic as a structural analogy for a flow (investment) feeding a stock (capital) through a drifting timing kernel; the analogy motivates the specification but is not itself evidence.")
